WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.600
When will the record boom in corporate dealmaking
that began over five years ago come to an

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.600
end?

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.610
It’s a question that dealmakers, who make
millions advising on transactions, don’t

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.619
like to ponder too often.

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:25.599
Since 2014, more than $20tn worth of deals
have been reached in over 250,000 transactions.

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:29.689
There are a few big picture reasons why dealmaking
has thrived.

00:00:29.689 --> 00:00:32.579
Low interest rates make for cheaper borrowing.

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:37.460
Rising stock prices that allow for equity
to be used in deals.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.370
Economic growth that hasn’t necessarily
translated into earnings growth.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.730
And most importantly, a stable geopolitical
backdrop.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.059
Those are the conditions under which corporate
executives tend to feel the most confident

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:53.180
about buying or merging with another company.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.910
That is why you always hear dealmakers talking
about boardroom confidence as a leading indicator

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.120
of M&amp;A activity.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.960
The gains over the past years have survived
unexpected geopolitical events.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.000
That includes Britain’s vote to exit the
European Union in 2016, and later, the election

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.980
of Donald Trump to president of the United
States.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.070
Each has affected M&amp;A in its own way.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:23.430
The prospect of Brexit has forced UK companies
to prepare for the economic impact of Britain’s

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.140
potential withdrawal from the EU.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:31.150
The boom in outbound Chinese dealmaking, which
took place in the first stretch of the M&amp;A

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:36.430
wave has come crashing down with Donald Trump’s
trade war against China.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:41.220
China has responded by combining state-owned
companies to create national champions of

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.380
the future.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.340
But despite a few short-lived slowdowns in
activity, the blockbuster deals keep coming.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:53.590
That is particularly the case in the US, which
unlike Europe, keeps pumping out megadeals.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.770
One thing that could bring this deal party
to an end is a recession.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.810
This would have a big negative impact on transactions.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:06.390
After all, stability is probably the most
important ingredient for dealmaking.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:11.450
Think about it this way, nobody wants to buy
at the top of the market, just before valuations

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:11.950
collapse.

